# Releases

[Changesets](https://changesets.dev) computes versions; the pipeline is [`.github/workflows/release.yml`](../../../.github/workflows/release.yml) - read it for the job list. Human-facing "how do I": [`.changeset/README.md`](../../../.changeset/README.md); writing a changeset: [changesets.mdc](changesets.mdc). CI internals: [ci.mdc](ci.mdc); the deploy: [cd.mdc](cd.mdc).

Nothing is published to npm: every workspace is `private: true`, with no `publishConfig` and no `.npmrc`. **A release is a git tag plus a Cloudflare Workers promote.**

## State machine

```text
push to main ──► Release  (concurrency release-main, queue: max, never canceled)
  ├─ gate         ALWAYS. uses ci.yml (full graph, not --affected)
  ├─ select-mode  'version' | 'none'   ('publish' is unreachable here)
  ├─ mode == 'version' → version → open/update the "chore: release" PR
  │                                → dispatch ci.yml on changeset-release/main   [END]
  └─ mode != 'version' → tag (needs gate) → created? → deploy → uses cd.yml
```

## Invariants

- **`gate` runs on every push to main, in both modes.** While a release PR is open the mode is always `version`, so a gate only on the release path would leave `main` unvalidated for that PR's whole life. Never move it under the `mode != 'version'` branch.
- **The tag is the idempotency key.** `.github/actions/release/create-release-tag.sh` skips when `vX.Y.Z` exists and reports `created=false`; `deploy` is gated on `created == 'true'`, so re-running `Release` never re-deploys. The existence probe is three-way: 2xx skips, 404 creates, any other failure fails the job rather than guessing. Redeploy on purpose with `cd.yml`'s `workflow_dispatch` and the tag.
- **`mode != 'version'`, not `mode == 'publish'`**: `select-mode` returns `publish` only when publishable packages exist, and all of ours are private, so the equality test would never fire. Load-bearing.
- **`select-mode` installs first**: with no pending changeset, as on every push after a release PR merges, it runs `changeset publish-plan` through `require.resolve("@changesets/cli/bin.js")` from the checkout, so without `pnpm install` the job fails and no tag is cut. Only the `version` branch works without the CLI, which is why a missing install stays hidden while a release PR is open. Every package is private and `privatePackages.tag` is `false`, so the plan is empty and the mode is `none`.
- **`privatePackages.tag: false` is deliberate**: `changeset git-tag` (v3's name for `changeset tag`) would emit one `<app>@X.Y.Z` per app, while the deploy keys on one shared `vX.Y.Z`, which `create-release-tag.sh` cuts instead.
- **`fixed: [["*"]]` is the app set, resting on apps being unscoped**: Changesets matches each `fixed` entry with picomatch against package **names**, and `*` does not cross `/`, so the group is exactly the unscoped workspaces (the apps), never an `@repo/*` package; a new app joins the shared version by existing. Nothing machine-checks the naming underneath (`.syncpackrc.json`'s `@repo/**` group governs dependency specifiers, not workspace names; `pnpm boundaries` never reads a name), so [naming.mdc](../quality/naming.mdc) keeps it by hand: an unscoped package under `packages/` would silently join the group and bump to the app version.
- **`changelog` names no `repo`**: `@changesets/changelog-github` falls back to `GITHUB_REPOSITORY`, which Actions sets in the `version` job, so a repository created from the template links its own PRs and commits. Hard-coding the slug would point every derived changelog at this repository. `changeset version` runs only in CI, so nothing local needs it.
- **`privatePackages.version: true` is load-bearing**: v3 still defaults to `{version: false, tag: false}`, which would version nothing here. `baseBranch: "main"` restates the v3 default and stays because the bundled `changesets/action` sub-actions read the config with their own copy of `@changesets/config`.
- **`changedFilePatterns` is the "what ships" list**, matched with picomatch against paths relative to each package directory, so `!*.md` drops only top-level docs (`README.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`), never a Markdown file under `src/`. It has no `dot` option: dotfiles never count, so a shipped dotfile (`public/.well-known/*`) needs its own pattern. Keep it aligned with the excludes of turbo's `transit` task.
- **`ignore: ["@repo/vitest-config"]`**: that package is test tooling only, so a change to it never needs a changeset. The config rule that dependents of an ignored package must be ignored too skips devDependency edges, so the apps stay versionable. Never ignore a package an app bundles: its changes would stop requiring a changeset.
- **`format: false`**: `"auto"` detects oxfmt, which ignores `**/*.md`, so it only spawned a no-op; changesets and changelogs are never reformatted ([markdown-style.mdc](../quality/markdown-style.mdc)). `$schema` pins the installed `@changesets/config` version; bump it with `@changesets/cli`.
- **`release:check` ([changesets/check.mjs](../../../.github/actions/changesets/check.mjs), its logic `checkChangesets` in [changesets/lib.mjs](../../../.github/actions/changesets/lib.mjs)) wraps `changeset status --since=origin/main --output`**, which exits 1 only when packages changed and **no** changeset changed since the merge base, and counts only tracked files (`git diff`), so a new changeset must be staged. Plain `changeset status` (`release:status`) counts every pending changeset, so it cannot tell whether the branch added one. The JSON plan feeds the lint (app-only names, title length, headings, `major` behind `CHANGESET_ALLOW_MAJOR`). Editing any pending changeset satisfies the presence half: coarse by design, the lint and review cover the rest.
- **The changeset PR comment renders the gate's own result** ([changesets/comment.mjs](../../../.github/actions/changesets/comment.mjs)): `changeset-pr-status.yml` runs `checkChangesets`, so the comment and the CI **Changesets** step never disagree, and `changesets/action/pr-comment` only posts it under the default marker. Never go back to `changesets/action/pr-status`: it takes no inputs, its add-a-changeset link prefills every changed workspace package (`@repo/*` and ignored ones included, which `release:check` rejects or Changesets refuses as a mixed changeset), an empty changeset reads as "included in the next version bump", and its no-changeset text assumes a non-blocking setup. The job installs because `changeset status` needs the CLI; it restores the pnpm store read-only ([ci.mdc](ci.mdc)). `changeset status` exits 1 both for a missing changeset and for a crash, so a non-zero exit with no `.changeset/*.md` changed since the merge base reads as missing, anything else as unavailable: the renderer exits 1 after writing that body, and `pr-comment` runs on `!cancelled()`, so a failed run replaces the comment instead of leaving the previous commit's.
- **Catalog bumps are checked separately** ([changesets/lib.mjs](../../../.github/actions/changesets/lib.mjs)): `pnpm-workspace.yaml` belongs to no package, so Changesets never sees a catalog change. `runtimeCatalogChanges` diffs the `catalog:` / `catalogs:` specifiers against the merge base and keeps those an app lists under `dependencies`. A bump with no changeset in the branch fails the gate. In Actions the merge-base `pnpm-workspace.yaml` is read through the contents API with the job's read-only `GITHUB_TOKEN`, never `git show`: the `blob:none` checkout would fetch that blob lazily without credentials, which fails on a private repository such as one created from this template. Locally it is `git show`. Lockfile-only refreshes of a direct dependency are not detected; add lockfile importer diffing if they start to matter.
- **Dependabot changesets** ([dependabot-changesets.yml](../../../.github/workflows/dependabot-changesets.yml)) commit one `patch` changeset per affected app as `.changeset/dependabot-<PR>-<app>.md`, reconciled to the desired state on every run, so a run that finds them current is a no-op. That no-op path needs no token, which matters because the run our own push triggers gets Actions secrets rather than Dependabot secrets.
  - The commit goes through GraphQL `createCommitOnBranch` with `expectedHeadOid`: GitHub signs it, and it fails rather than overwrite a newer push.
  - It uses the `CHANGESET_BOT_TOKEN` Dependabot secret (a fine-grained PAT, Contents: write), because Dependabot runs get a read-only `GITHUB_TOKEN` and a `GITHUB_TOKEN` push would start no CI run.
  - `[dependabot skip]` in the message keeps Dependabot rebasing the PR; a rebase drops the commit and the next run writes it again.
  - `pull_request`, never `pull_request_target`, and never `pnpm install` in that job: only git and Node run while the token is in the environment. With the secret unset it prints a notice and exits 0, and `ci.yml` sets `CHANGESET_SKIP_RUNTIME_DEPENDENCIES=1` for that Dependabot PR, so the gate warns instead of failing: a project created from the template works with no setup, and its Dependabot bumps ship without a release note until someone adds one. The flag reads the Dependabot secret store, which is what a Dependabot-triggered run sees.
- **The `version` job dispatches CI on the release branch** (`actions: write`, beside the `contents` / `pull-requests: write` that `changesets/action/version` needs): the release PR is opened with `GITHUB_TOKEN`, so its `pull_request` runs wait for approval and the required check comes only from that dispatch ([ci.mdc](ci.mdc)). While a release PR is open, every push to `main` therefore runs the full graph twice, as `gate` and as the dispatch.
- **One contract comment on the release PR** ([release/comment-release-pr-contract.sh](../../../.github/actions/release/comment-release-pr-contract.sh)): `changesets/action/version` has no PR-body input, so its "published to npm automatically" description stays and the comment corrects it. `gh pr comment --edit-last --create-if-none` keeps one comment across every `version` re-run; `changesets/action/pr-comment` cannot be used here because it only targets the PR that triggered the run.
- **The version PR branch is force-pushed, not accumulated**: every push to `main` resets `changeset-release/main` from the tip, re-runs `changeset version`, and force-pushes one commit, so it is never behind or in conflict with `main`, and manual edits to it are destroyed.
- **`release.yml` calls `cd.yml` directly** because tags created with `GITHUB_TOKEN` start no workflow ([cd.mdc](cd.mdc)); never "restore" a tag trigger.
- **`queue: max`, not the default**: with `queue: single`, a third push replaces the pending run and that release is silently dropped.

## Recovery

| Fails | Recovery |
|-------|----------|
| `gate` red | Fix on a PR. No tag, no deploy. |
| `version` push rejected | Re-run; the branch is reset from `main` each time. |
| Release PR waits on the required check | The dispatch failed or predates the head commit: `gh workflow run ci.yml --ref changeset-release/main`. Never approve the held `pull_request` runs. |
| `tag` version drift | Re-align every app in one commit; the action fails closed, naming each app and its version. |
| Tag already exists | `created=false`, deploy skipped. Not an error. |
| `release:check` fails on a Dependabot PR | `CHANGESET_BOT_TOKEN` is set but expired or lacks access (the Dependabot changesets job is red): renew it, or add `pnpm changeset --patch <app> -m 'Bump …'` to the branch by hand. Without the secret the gate only warns. |
| `deploy` upload/promote/smoke | The tag stands. "Re-run failed jobs" on the Release run retries the deploy (the `tag` job's `created=true` output is preserved; "re-run all jobs" re-probes, gets `created=false`, and skips - safe, not a retry), or run `cd.yml` via `workflow_dispatch` with the tag. Rollback: `pnpm --filter=<app> exec cf workers deployments create --worker <app>-production --strategy percentage --versions '[{"version_id":"<previous id>","percentage":100}]'`, the previous id from `cf workers deployments list --worker <app>-production`. |

## Repo settings this depends on

- *Actions → General → Allow GitHub Actions to create and approve pull requests* enabled.
- The `production` GitHub Environment holding `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` / `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` and the `VITE_API_BASE_URL` variable; CD stays paused until they exist and `CD_ENABLED` is `'true'` ([cd.mdc](cd.mdc)).
- Sentry (optional): the `SENTRY_ORG` variable arms two CD steps per app - `sentry:sourcemaps` between upload and promote, and `sentry:deploy` (set-commits, finalize, deploy marker) after the production smoke. The split is load-bearing: Sentry resolves a `Fixes <SHORT-ID>` issue when set-commits links its commit to a release, so set-commits before promote would resolve issues before the fix is live. Both need the `SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN` secret and Sentry project slugs equal to the app names; set-commits also needs this repository in the Sentry GitHub integration. Both are `continue-on-error`, so a Sentry outage never blocks a release. `worker-api` also needs its `SENTRY_DSN` Worker secret set once on `worker-api-production` (a `bindings.secret()`), or the upload fails and nothing is promoted.
- Before real traffic: the `production` deployment's `corsOrigins` in `apps/worker-api/cloudflare.config.ts` ships empty, so `/api/*` fails closed with 503 and the CD smoke fails *after* both promotes. Setting it takes a new release - text bindings ship inside the uploaded version, so a CD re-run cannot fix it.
